ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [
  {system: "LLMCompiler", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04511 / https://github.com/SqueezeAILab/LLMCompiler", overlap: "High overlap on parallel function calling, but via plan-time DAG/variable references; not post-hoc independence detection. Reports up to 3.7x latency speedup, 6.7x cost saving, ~9% accuracy improvement vs ReAct."},
  {system: "OpenAI parallel tool/function calling", url: "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/function-calling", overlap: "Shipping API can emit multiple tool calls, but does not provide a general stateful dependency detector or correctness guarantee for post-hoc parallelization."},
  {system: "LangGraph", url: "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/workflows-agents", overlap: "Graph/workflow framework supports explicit parallel branches and stateful orchestration; overlap is explicit graph design, not automatic detector FNR measurement."},
  {system: "vLLM", url: "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm", overlap: "Inference serving engine: batching, KV cache, tool-call parsers; no found shipping mechanism for agent tool dependency detection."},
  {system: "SGLang", url: "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang", overlap: "Inference serving/runtime system: RadixAttention, batching, structured outputs; no found post-hoc agent tool dependency detector."},
  {system: "LMCache", url: "https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache", overlap: "KV-cache reuse/sharing for LLM serving; no overlap with tool-call dependency detection."},
  {system: "FlashInfer", url: "https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer", overlap: "GPU inference kernels for attention/GEMM/MoE; no overlap with agent tool scheduling correctness."},
  {system: "TensorRT-LLM", url: "https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM", overlap: "Optimized inference runtime/kernels and agent-app optimization material; no found mechanism matching post-hoc stateful tool dependency detection."}
]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: Holds enough for this systems claim as a negative result: BFCL v4 gives real multi-tool traces, 257 within-turn DAGs, 440 parallel instances, and three detectors including an H100-run Qwen2.5-1.5B judge. Gaps: BFCL parallel f_indep=1.0 is a curated ceiling, not a production workload mix; full agent-loop re-execution was not done; frontier-model dependency judging remains untested.
RATIONALE: Prompt version v001. This is not a new shipping mechanism: LLMCompiler already implements the practical escape hatch by constructing an explicit plan/DAG, and LangGraph-style systems expose explicit graph parallelism. The systems contribution is the measured negative insight that post-hoc detect-then-parallelize is unsafe on real stateful traces: detector FNR e=0.164 even for the best state-aware heuristic and 0.582/0.615 for value-flow/LLM detectors, all above the stated e<=0.025 safety threshold; empirical corruption is 2.4-8.5% per call and 3-17% per sequence. That is enough to reject the original “free latency win without correctness change” claim and explain why existing successful systems use plan-time DAG structure rather than after-the-fact dependency detection.
